urce_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320","cover_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48320/pg48320.cover.medium.jpg","formats":[{"type":"html","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.html.images"},{"type":"epub","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.epub3.images"},{"type":"mobi","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.kf8.images"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.txt.utf-8"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48320/48320-0.txt"}],"access":"public_domain","license":"Public domain in the United States","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"48320","gutenberg_id":"48320"},"providers":[{"source":"Project Gutenberg","source_id":"48320","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"html","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.html.images"},{"type":"epub","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.epub3.images"},{"type":"mobi","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.kf8.images"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48320.txt.utf-8"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48320/48320-0.txt"}],"access":"public_domain","license":"Public domain in the United States","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:a83f85f0cbd9c50583918c31","title":"Africa","subtitle":"","description":"This volume explores secularity in Sub-Saharan Africa, an area long considered to be, to quote John Mbiti, “notoriously religious”, or lurking in the secular shadows. Our sources question these assumptions by showing how contemporary secularities in Africa emerged from specific socio-cultural histories, and political conflicts and contestations, as well as from colonial impositions and African interventions. Organised thematically, the volume begins by showing how the constitutions of African post-colonial states regulate religion, with introductions providing the historical and political contexts of the specific configurations. The next sections deal with religionisation and culturalisation – the basic processes of dealing with the African past, and of reading African indigenous concepts, vocabularies, practices, and symbols, within the framework of religious-secular distinctions. We present sources debating the separation of religion and politics from African socialism, atheist organisations, and public debates. Two further sections deal with expressions of secularity in African literature and photography. The last three sections share a regional focus on southern Africa, but tell rather different stories about the colonial formation and subsequent transformations of secularity.","authors":[],"language":"en","year":2025,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Africa","Secularity","Religion","Politics","Afrika","Säkularismus","Politik","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRY Alternative belief systems::QRYA Humanist and secular alternatives to religion","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRM Christianity","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRA Religion: general::QRAX History of religion"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116833","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/cc6f25cf-6139-4c32-ad0d-6c4d0d2b954d/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/0a478d85-207b-4a72-9ac4-a11d72274572/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116833","handle":"20.500.12657/116833","dspace_uuid":"e5f97432-7bac-46a9-b552-82f89736c51f"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116833","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116833","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/0a478d85-207b-4a72-9ac4-a11d72274572/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:20ff4c638ea253b1d7160ffa","title":"African Storytelling and Images in Teaching","subtitle":"Youth and Crisis Management through Folktales and Visuals from Kenya and Ethiopia","description":"As a team of upcoming and established scholars from Kenya, Ethiopia and Belgium, we propose this book with oral folktales accompanied by strong visuals. Through performative learning procedures the book invites young people to meaningfully engage with living traditions of oral storytelling. The book aims to: 1) (re-)value the educational potential of popular storytelling in dealing with crisis situations; 2) (re)connect adolescent audiences with oral literatures; As the interest in oral literatures is on the decline, especially among adolescents in Africa and elsewhere, we see a real need in offering folktales that are attractive and relevant to youthful audiences. The book features 11 oral narratives in 5 languages from Kenya and Ethiopia, accompanied by high-quality images that will speak to young people. Each chapter starts with a full transcription of the story in the original language, followed by a well-written and attractive translation to English. The visual section highlights the narrative’s features in different manners, after which metadata on the narrative are offered, explaining the performance context, background of the narrator and audience. The final section of each chapter consists of a profound analysis with full educational guidelines for teachers/trainers to engage discussion with (African) youth.","authors":[],"language":"en","year":2026,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["African Folktales","Intergenerational Connections","Visual Storytelling","Crisis Management","Afrikanische Sagen und Märchen","Intergenerationelle Verbindungen","Visuelles Geschichtenerzählen","Krisenmanagement","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRA Religion: general","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology::NH History::NHH African history","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRA Religion: general::QRAC Comparative religion"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116932","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/7720de43-cd3e-4ce9-a539-cb9958305681/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/6fb61a72-5909-4a0e-b6a9-86e8defa7325/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116932","handle":"20.500.12657/116932","dspace_uuid":"9b9ae410-2bc3-4af6-889c-8c3dd2fdfa95"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116932","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116932","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/6fb61a72-5909-4a0e-b6a9-86e8defa7325/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:fa7f785810cfa83b803938b2","title":"African Storytelling and Images in Teaching","subtitle":"Youth and Crisis Management through Folktales and Visuals from Kenya and Ethiopia","description":"As a team of upcoming and established scholars from Kenya, Ethiopia and Belgium, we propose this book with oral folktales accompanied by strong visuals. Through performative learning procedures the book invites young people to meaningfully engage with living traditions of oral storytelling. The book aims to: 1) (re-)value the educational potential of popular storytelling in dealing with crisis situations; 2) (re)connect adolescent audiences with oral literatures; As the interest in oral literatures is on the decline, especially among adolescents in Africa and elsewhere, we see a real need in offering folktales that are attractive and relevant to youthful audiences. The book features 11 oral narratives in 5 languages from Kenya and Ethiopia, accompanied by high-quality images that will speak to young people. Each chapter starts with a full transcription of the story in the original language, followed by a well-written and attractive translation to English. The visual section highlights the narrative’s features in different manners, after which metadata on the narrative are offered, explaining the performance context, background of the narrator and audience. The final section of each chapter consists of a profound analysis with full educational guidelines for teachers/trainers to engage discussion with (African) youth.","authors":[],"language":"en","year":2026,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["African Folktales","Intergenerational Connections","Visual Storytelling","Crisis Management","Afrikanische Sagen und Märchen","Intergenerationelle Verbindungen","Visuelles Geschichtenerzählen","Krisenmanagement","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRA Religion: general","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology::NH History::NHH African history","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRA Religion: general::QRAC Comparative religion"],"source":"DOAB","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181507","cover_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/rest/bitstreams/3d03e045-aeaf-47a4-81be-24ff933cf18e/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116932/1/9783112235010.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12854/181507","handle":"20.500.12854/181507","dspace_uuid":"195fba02-3a04-4526-b08e-7007cc1074fa"},"providers":[{"source":"DOAB","source_id":"20.500.12854/181507","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181507","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116932/1/9783112235010.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:8e59798015800db5404392d4","title":"Agricultura inteligente: rendimiento y calidad del melón criollo (Cucumis melo L.) variedad Dudaim con abonos ecológicos","subtitle":"","description":"This book invites a reconsideration of agricultural production paradigms in a context where consumers demand high-quality food cultivated under environmentally responsible practices. Focusing on the Dudaim variety, considered a highly valuable agricultural heritage, the work demonstrates how the use of compost, bokashi, and biol not only increases crop productivity but also strengthens the sustainability of agricultural systems. The study was conducted in the canton of Buena Fe, Los Ríos province, Ecuador, a region recognized for its productive richness and as a privileged setting for innovation and tradition. The research, designed under a rigorous experimental model, yields revealing results. Compost enhances vegetative growth and fruit weight, bokashi improves pulp quality and melon diameter, while biol increases sweetness and soluble solids, a key attribute for meeting increasingly demanding markets. These findings go beyond the academic sphere, providing farmers with practical tools to decide which fertilizer to use according to their production goals, optimizing resources and reducing dependence on chemical fertilizers that have degraded soils and increased costs. More than a technical text, this book is a manifesto for smart agriculture, capable of balancing tradition, science, and sustainability. From the agricultural heart of Los Ríos, it demonstrates that the Dudaim melon can drive competitiveness, resilience, and cultural pride. In this way, the work charts an inspiring path toward profitable and environmentally respectful production systems.","authors":["Murillo-Orellana, Diego Djorkaeff","Espinosa-Carillo, José Francisco"],"language":"es","year":2025,"publisher":"Sophia Editions","isbn":[],"doi":"10.64092/abtn6229","subjects":["Agricultura sostenible","Abonos orgánicos","Melón Dudaim","Calidad del fruto","Innovación agrícola","thema EDItEUR::T Technology, Engineering, Agriculture, Industrial processes::TV Agriculture and farming::TVF Sustainable agriculture"],"source":"DOAB","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/178536.2","cover_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/rest/bitstreams/fca0bb2f-5eb1-4105-b1d9-89472e24465b/retrieve","formats":[],"access":"open_access","license":"Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12854/178536.2","handle":"20.500.12854/178536.2","dspace_uuid":"50b41b79-4608-4b61-ac84-60f79f6acc2b"},"providers":[{"source":"DOAB","source_id":"20.500.12854/178536.2","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/178536.2","language":"es","formats":[],"access":"open_access","license":"Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:433715c5ae22a1ea64b0441b","title":"aha! Insight","subtitle":"","description":"","authors":["Martin Gardner"],"language":"en","year":1978,"publisher":"Tubitak Yayinlari","isbn":["2902918879","9780716710172","9754034826","8433551256","9780894540011","0894540017","9782902918874","9788433551252","9789576212802","9789754034820","071671017X","9576212804"],"doi":"","subjects":[],"source":"Open Library","source_url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL460031W","cover_url":"h