":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116873","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116873","language":"it","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/05883fd8-1689-483d-9b3d-2f4dc3e2f573/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:959cad84ae9512714e3e7b8e","title":"Epistolario di Giovanni Comisso","subtitle":"","description":"This volume presents a commented edition of the letters of Italian writer Giovanni Comisso (1895–1969), that is more than 1,000 documents, mostly unpublished to date. These documents constitute an invaluable source for the study of this author and the vibrant literary and cultural context of his time.","authors":[],"language":"it","year":2025,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Giovanni Comisso","20th-Century Italian Literature","Letters","Edition","Comisso","Giovanni","Italienische Literatur","20. Jahrhundert","Briefwerk","thema EDItEUR::D Biography, Literature and Literary studies::DS Literature: history and criticism::DSB Literary studies: general","thema EDItEUR::D Biography, Literature and Literary studies::DS Literature: history and criticism"],"source":"DOAB","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181413","cover_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/rest/bitstreams/52131a60-6776-41b1-9b15-c1cee87a25e9/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116873/1/9783111437859.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12854/181413","handle":"20.500.12854/181413","dspace_uuid":"f0b95dbd-fa9f-47b7-93cc-8d7b98aa32f8"},"providers":[{"source":"DOAB","source_id":"20.500.12854/181413","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181413","language":"it","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116873/1/9783111437859.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:379434d8d83d320ffd3992b4","title":"Erasing and Rewriting in Manuscript Cultures","subtitle":"Practices of Text Obliteration and Manuscript Reuse in a Global Perspective","description":"Across the most diverse societies, the reuse of writing surfaces in contexts such as learning to write, note-taking, literary creation, and bookkeeping has been not only desirable but essential to optimising resources. In these contexts, it has been typical to use erasable media of various kinds – a practice which in turn has had important implications for palaeography, literary creation, and virtually every aspect related to writing. Manuscript cultures addressed the quest for rewritability through using permanently recyclable materials such as clay and wax tablets, special technologies such as erasable coatings, and ingenious strategies for reusing papyrus, parchment, paper, wood, and more. This volume systematically explores the fascinating intersection between the potential ephemerality of the written word and the reusability of its supports. It combines a theoretical framework on ‘rewritability’ with case studies on materials, related technologies and their interplay with competing techniques, spanning from ancient Mesopotamia to present-day Nigeria, from Egypt to Japan, from Greece and Rome to the Arab world. In doing so, the volume illuminates a crucial aspect of cultural history relevant to anyone studying the written word.","authors":[],"language":"en","year":2025,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Writing technologies","Erasure techniques","Recycling manuscripts","Palimpsests_x000D_","Handschriftenkunde","Schrift+Technologie","Recycling","Palimpsest","thema EDItEUR::D Biography, Literature and Literary studies::DS Literature: history and criticism::DSB Literary studies: general","thema EDItEUR::D Biography, Literature and Literary studies::DS Literature: history and criticism","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology::NH History::NHD European history"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116853","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/102e6f1b-6bcc-4799-8583-2951e56984f6/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/5260009d-9839-4360-85ed-ef97a82b1fed/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116853","handle":"20.500.12657/116853","dspace_uuid":"b8b39593-e6e7-4040-a69c-d99b08aae8cb"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116853","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116853","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/5260009d-9839-4360-85ed-ef97a82b1fed/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:df3868bdd48e3d78482a9604","title":"Erasing and Rewriting in Manuscript Cultures","subtitle":"Practices of Text Obliteration and Manuscript Reuse in a Global Perspective","description":"Across the most diverse societies, the reuse of writing surfaces in contexts such as learning to write, note-taking, literary creation, and bookkeeping has been not only desirable but essential to optimising resources. In these contexts, it has been typical to use erasable media of various kinds – a practice which in turn has had important implications for palaeography, literary creation, and virtually every aspect related to writing. Manuscript cultures addressed the quest for rewritability through using permanently recyclable materials such as clay and wax tablets, special technologies such as erasable coatings, and ingenious strategies for reusing papyrus, parchment, paper, wood, and more. This volume systematically explores the fascinating intersection between the potential ephemerality of the written word and the reusability of its supports. It combines a theoretical framework on ‘rewritability’ with case studies on materials, related technologies and their interplay with competing techniques, spanning from ancient Mesopotamia to present-day Nigeria, from Egypt to Japan, from Greece and Rome to the Arab world. In doing so, the volume illuminates a crucial aspect of cultural history relevant to anyone studying the written word.","authors":[],"language":"en","year":2025,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Writing technologies","Erasure techniques","Recycling manuscripts","Palimpsests_x000D_","Handschriftenkunde","Schrift+Technologie","Recycling","Palimpsest","thema EDItEUR::D Biography, Literature and Literary studies::DS Literature: history and criticism::DSB Literary studies: general","thema EDItEUR::D Biography, Literature and Literary studies::DS Literature: history and criticism","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology::NH History::NHD European history"],"source":"DOAB","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181563","cover_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/rest/bitstreams/31dc404f-7a79-46fa-9219-0f066562d2a2/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116853/1/9783111682983.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12854/181563","handle":"20.500.12854/181563","dspace_uuid":"78e093f5-2857-417f-8ac2-2f1ababf7588"},"