e housing development, supplemented by portraits of current female residents, to illuminate their lived environment and personal experiences. With a contribution by Nina Hüppi Women as users and protagonists of modernist architecture Interviews with contemporary residents as a research and teaching method Shows the discrepancy between male construction and female use","authors":[],"language":"de","year":2025,"publisher":"jovis Verlag","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Gender studies","Women’s history","Women’s studies","Cooperative","Equality","Modernism","Oral history","Switzerland","Werkbund","Housing","Zurich","Neubühl","Werkbund housing estate","New Building","Housing development","Zurich Werkbund","Bern University of Applied Sciences","Male construction","Female use","Architecture and gender","Schweiz","Werkbundsiedlung","Neues Bauen","Siedlungsbau","Werkbund Zürich","Klassische Moderne","Berner Fachhochschule","Wohnungsbau","Architektur und Gender","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AM Architecture::AMX History of architecture","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AM Architecture::AMB Individual architects and architectural firms"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116938","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/c1fb2a9b-6afb-4d8f-9057-59b8b62e9dc1/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/3bd0d910-754e-489e-bbc5-570692996216/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116938","handle":"20.500.12657/116938","dspace_uuid":"4eab072a-b89f-44b1-b994-36c50afa319e"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116938","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116938","language":"de","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/3bd0d910-754e-489e-bbc5-570692996216/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:edb28dbdccc460e4bbd66456","title":"Frauen leben im Neubühl","subtitle":"","description":"\"Living here is like life on a permanent holiday,” reads an advertisement, published in 1932, for the newly inaugurated Werkbund housing development Neubühl in Zurich. Even today, with its intelligently conceived outdoor spaces, residential infrastructure, and compact, rational floor plans, it remains a significant living example of the New Building ( Neues Bauen ) in Switzerland. It is still so beloved by its residents that many spend most of their lives there. This is made possible by the skillful integration of a variety of housing types—from small studios to six-room townhouses. In their book, the editors compilecontributions on the history, typology, and significance of the housing development, supplemented by portraits of current female residents, to illuminate their lived environment and personal experiences. With a contribution by Nina Hüppi Women as users and protagonists of modernist architecture Interviews with contemporary residents as a research and teaching method Shows the discrepancy between male construction and female use","authors":[],"language":"de","year":2025,"publisher":"jovis Verlag","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Gender studies","Women’s history","Women’s studies","Cooperative","Equality","Modernism","Oral history","Switzerland","Werkbund","Housing","Zurich","Neubühl","Werkbund housing estate","New Building","Housing development","Zurich Werkbund","Bern University of Applied Sciences","Male construction","Female use","Architecture and gender","Schweiz","Werkbundsiedlung","Neues Bauen","Siedlungsbau","Werkbund Zürich","Klassische Moderne","Berner Fachhochschule","Wohnungsbau","Architektur und Gender","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AM Architecture::AMX History of architecture","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AM Architecture::AMB Individual architects and architectural firms"],"source":"DOAB","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181384","cover_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/rest/bitstreams/cc0b0eb4-90d2-4b55-9b8f-b405377496f2/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116938/1/9783986122003.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12854/181384","handle":"20.500.12854/181384","dspace_uuid":"7011fe74-34ac-4baa-bcc5-97658c4f2e00"},"providers":[{"source":"DOAB","source_id":"20.500.12854/181384","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181384","language":"de","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116938/1/9783986122003.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:44c18d2c2037039dc1a5597b","title":"Freedom of Religion and the Relativity of Human Rights","subtitle":"","description":"Human rights are a central common denominator in global politics – but they often only fulfil this function if cultural relativity is ignored. In this volume, we use the example of the human right to freedom of religion (Universal Declaration of Human Rights, Art. 18) to show that human rights are rooted in Latin-European concepts and are therefore culturally relative. We discuss this relativity with regard to interpretations of religious freedom in Buddhist, Hindu, Islamic and Christian cultures. These examples demonstrate that our understanding of human rights is merely a product of negotiation. This, in turn, has enormous consequences for politics: We cannot assume that cultures which do not share Latin-European concepts, that are closely linked to Christian ideas, will adopt the human rights concepts without revision. The Declaration of Human Rights is therefore not a quasi-metaphysical foundation of politics, as we might wish, but the result of committed negotiation processes. Only an understanding of human rights that integrates the fundamental differences in the history and justifications of the freedom of religion into the political debate is capable of establishing a consensus on the validity of human rights.","authors":["Willems, Joachim","Zander, Helmut"],"language":"en","year":2026,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Religionsfreiheit","Menschenrechte","Geschichte","Freedom of religion","Human rights","History","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRA Religion: general::QRAC Comparative religion","thema EDItEUR::J Society and Social Sciences::JP Politics and government::JPV Political control and freedoms::JPVH Human rights, civil rights"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116923","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/4542b760-0c13-42ca-a846-cd59f9b403b2/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/86424c21-77fe-48e7-af87-77ed414f94fc/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116923","handle":"20.500.12657/116923","dspace_uuid":"205e9ba0-a6a7-446b-a32b-4b7864ed4eae"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116923","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116923","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/86424c21-77fe-48e7-af87-77ed414f94fc/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:40c3f2485f10b3e8366e7e85","title":"Friday, the Thirteenth: A Novel","subtitle":"","description":"","authors":["Lawson, Thomas William"],"language":"en","year":null,"publisher":"Project Gutenberg","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Banks and banking, American -- Fiction","Banks and banking, International -- Fiction","Financial crises -- Fiction","National banks (United States) -- Fiction","Stock exchanges -- United States -- Fiction","Category: American Literature","Category: Crime, Thrillers and Mystery","Category: Novels"],"source":"Project Gutenberg","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345","cover_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12345/pg12345.cover.medium.jpg","formats":[{"type":"html","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.html.images"},{"type":"epub","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.epub3.images"},{"type":"mobi","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.kf8.images"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.txt.utf-8"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/12345/12345-0.txt"}],"access":"public_domain","license":"Public domain in the United States","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"12345","gutenberg_id":"12345"},"providers":[{"source":"Project Gutenberg","source_id":"12345","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"html","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.html.images"},{"type":"epub","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.epub3.images"},{"type":"mobi","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.kf8.images"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12345.txt.utf-8"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/12345/12345-0.txt"}],"access":"public_domain","license":"Public domain in the United States","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:d749b124cba21d89360e5f0f","title":"From Algorithms to Evidence","subtitle":"Using GenAI in Evaluation Practice","description":"From Algorithms to Evidence: Using GenAI in Evaluation Practice offers a timely, practice-grounded guide for evaluators and development professionals navigating the fast-moving world of generative AI. Building on the foundations laid in Artificial Intelligence and Evaluation (2025), this volume moves decisively from theory to application. It documents how evaluators across the globe are already using GenAI in real projects, showing not only what worked but also what failed, why, and under what conditions. The result is a clear, practitioner-centered resource that cuts through hype and provides grounded, real-world insight. Structured around the seven phases of the evaluation lifecycle (design, structuring and inception, data collection, data analysis, reporting, judgment, and utilization), this book offers concrete examples of how GenAI is being integrated into everyday evaluation tasks. Contributors explain their rationale for using AI tools, the steps they took, and the results they achieved. Crosscutting chapters synthesize lessons on methodological adaptation, evolving evaluator competencies, and the ethical and professional standards needed to use GenAI responsibly. Throughout, this volume emphasizes ‘hybrid intelligence,’ showing how human expertise and AI-enabled methods can work together to strengthen evaluative reasoning. Clear, accessible, and grounded in real practice, From Algorithms to Evidence fills a critical gap in the literature. It provides evaluators, policymakers, and organizational leaders with practical guidance for building more adaptive, data-informed, and future-ready evaluation systems while keeping equity, transparency, and human judgment at the center of their work.","authors":[],"language":"en","year":2026,"publisher":"Taylor & Francis","isbn":[],"doi":"10.4324/9781003799139","subjects":["Program Evaluation","Artificial Intelligence","Digital Technologies","Governance","Big