,"source_id":"OL362702W","source_url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL362702W","language":"es","formats":[{"type":"read","url":"https://archive.org/details/shakespeare-giulio-cesare-angeli"}],"access":"open_access","license":"","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:dbe285db7449a705eeac730c","title":"Julius Streicher – Tainted Images, Stolen Lives","subtitle":"The Anti-Semitic Tabloid ‘Der Stürmer’ and Children’s Readers","description":"In Leni Riefenstahl's propaganda film of 1934 Nazi Party Rally in Nuremberg ( Triumph of the Will ), Julius Streicher proclaims: “A people which does not hold with the purity of its race will perish!” This belief will consume Streicher’s life until his death on the gallows in 1946. Streicher, a devoted disciple of Hitler from 1923 at the time of the aborted Beer Hall Putsch , held some of the most anti-Semitic beliefs of the Nazi Party. After Dr. Joseph Goebbels, Minister of Propaganda and Public Enlightenment, Streicher became perhaps the next highly established propagandist in the Nazi Party, although berated for his crude and simplistic means of spreading the hatred of Jews. In his diary, Joseph Goebbels himself decried Streicher’s outlandish caricatures. From 1923 to 1945, Streicher published his infamous anti-Semitic weekly newspaper Der Stürmer. The newspaper, filled with vulgar stereotypes of Jews, at times bordering on the semi-pornographic, emanated from Julius Streicher'spublishing house and was read religiously in the popular display cases throughout Germany. In 1936, following the pedagogical guidelines of “education” of children in the Nazi ideology, Streicher’s publishing house originated and distributed three anti-Semitic children’s books: Trust No Fox on His Green Heath and No Jew on His Oath, The Poisonous Mushroom and The Mongrel . The propagandistic goal of the books was to teach children at an early age to hate Jews, seen as the scourge of the German Aryan population. A detailed analysis of the images by various illustrators such as Willi Hofmann and Philipp Rupprecht (Fips) reveal the Jew as “The Other”, which helped reinforce the hatred of Jews, leading to the tragedy of the Holocaust. A most valuable contribution to this volume was made by the foremost specialist in Streicher and Rupprecht's images, Ralph Keysers, who graciously contributed previously unpublished images.","authors":["Michalczyk, John J."],"language":"en","year":2025,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Antisemitism","Holocaust","Stereotypes","Nuremberg Trials_x000D_","Antisemitismus","Nürnberger Prozesse","Stereotyp","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QR Religion and beliefs::QRJ Judaism","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology::NH History::NHT History: specific events and topics::NHTB Social and cultural history","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology::NH History::NHT History: specific events and topics::NHTZ Genocide and ethnic cleansing::NHTZ1 The Holocaust","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology::NH History"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116852","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/4d8a624c-657b-48df-917e-61f0ffb17ee7/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/ed07fb51-0cb9-488e-b834-8023dbe2aced/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116852","handle":"20.500.12657/116852","dspace_uuid":"4239b957-b777-4588-8191-d5631ddfbcca"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116852","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116852","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/ed07fb51-0cb9-488e-b834-8023dbe2aced/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:a48d2daed77eae72c257e466","title":"Just Around the Corner: Romance en casserole","subtitle":"","description":"","authors":["Hurst, Fannie"],"language":"en","year":null,"publisher":"Project Gutenberg","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["New York (N.Y.) -- Fiction","Short stories","Category: American Literature","Category: Novels","Category: Romance"],"source":"Project Gutenberg","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118","cover_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/33118/pg33118.cover.medium.jpg","formats":[{"type":"html","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.html.images"},{"type":"epub","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.epub3.images"},{"type":"mobi","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.kf8.images"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.txt.utf-8"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/33118/33118-8.txt"}],"access":"public_domain","license":"Public domain in the United States","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"33118","gutenberg_id":"33118"},"providers":[{"source":"Project Gutenberg","source_id":"33118","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"html","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.html.images"},{"type":"epub","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.epub3.images"},{"type":"mobi","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.kf8.images"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/33118.txt.utf-8"},{"type":"text","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/33118/33118-8.txt"}],"access":"public_domain","license":"Public domain in the United States","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:c246ee287e5f1e9a7e77acbb","title":"Kant und die Antike","subtitle":"","description":"Das Verhältnis von Kants Philosophie zum Denken der Antike ist Gegenstand einer anhaltenden Kontroverse. Einerseits wurde im Sinne der querelle des anciens et des modernes behauptet, dass sich Kants Denken aus spezifisch neuzeitlichen Prämissen und Problemstellungen heraus entwickelt habe, sodass von einem Einfluss der antiken Philosophie auf das Denken Kants sensu stricto nicht gesprochen werden könne. Andererseits wurde immer wieder die besondere Bedeutung der antiken Philosophen und Philosophenschulen für die Genese des kantischen Systems herausgestellt. Vor allem Platon wurde dabei wiederholt als wichtige Inspirationsquelle genannt. Das Ziel des vorliegenden Bandes ist es, ein wenig Licht in dieses philosophiehistorische Dickicht zu bringen, indem Kant-Expert*innen und Antike-Forscher*innen miteinander in einen Austausch gebracht werden, um das Verhältnis von Kants Praktischer Philosophie zur antiken philosophischen Tradition anhand von einigen ausgewählten Themenkomplexen zu beleuchten. Im Mittelpunkt der Analysen der Autor*innen stehen zentrale Begrifflichkeiten der Individualethik Kants (Tugend, Glück, Pflicht), seiner Politischen Philosophie (Staatsbürgerschaft, Kosmopolitismus) sowie die Frage nach Kants philosophischer Methode.","authors":[],"language":"de","year":2025,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Kant","Immanuel","Antiquity","Ethics","Political philosophy","Antike","Ethik","Politische Philosophie","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QD Philosophy","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QD Philosophy::QDH Philosophical traditions and schools of thought","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QD Philosophy::QDH Philosophical traditions and schools of thought::QDHR Western philosophy from c 1800","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QD Philosophy::QDH Philosophical traditions and schools of thought::QDHA Ancient Greek and Roman philosophy","thema EDItEUR::Q Philosophy and Religion::QD Philosophy::QDT Topics in philosophy::QDTQ Ethics and moral philosophy"],"source":"DOAB","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181490","cover_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/rest/bitstreams/5802d5fe-ce04-4f4a-9f7e-dd3755d892d4/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116777/1/9783111444567.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12854/181490","handle":"20.500.12854/181490","dspace_uuid":"3742740f-6f0d-450f-8f14-e377e170c1e9"},"providers":[{"source":"DOAB","source_id":"20.500.12854/181490","source_url":"https://directory.doabooks.org/handle/20.500.12854/181490","language":"de","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/116777/1/9783111444567.pdf"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"open access · https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:6d05b2bcc8453c1fd3a10df6","title":"Katzen und Kinder: Kopräsente Phänomene in virtuellen Meetings","subtitle":"Noticings als multimodale Praktiken der Raum- und Beziehungsherstellung","description":"Pathways to Multimodality offers an exciting new platform for innovative scholarship in multimodality building on a wide range of disciplines including, but not limited to, linguistics, media studies, visual communication, journalism, rhetoric, film and comic studies. In all of these fields and beyond, multimodality has become one of the most influential perspectives available for the study of communicative processes in which broad varieties of semiotic modes and resources, such as speech, writing, image, sound and many more, combine in specific media artefacts and interactions. Pathways to Multimodality invites monographs and edited volumes that explicitly address the multimodal nature of communication in all its depth and diversity. The series especially promotes empirical work that draws on larger sets of multimodal data and adopts qualitative, quantitative, or mixed-methods approaches as well as contributions advancing general aspects of multimodal theory and method. Clarity of presentation is a key priority as the series is addressed to a wide readership of both newcomers and experts across the full diversity of multimodal communities and caters for a broad variety of teaching, research, and application contexts. Editorial Board Jannis Androutsopoulos Arlene Archer Jeff Bezemer Neil Cohn Arnulf Deppermann Charles Forceville Ellen Fricke Tuomo Hiippala Carey Jewitt Jens E. Kjeldsen Silvia Kutscher Fei Victor Lim Martin Luginbühl Arianna Maiorani Konstanze Marx Lorenza Mondada Cornelia Müller Sigrid Norris Florence Oloff Asli Ozyurek Louise Ravelli Axel Schmidt Frank Serafini Jürgen Spitzmüller Dušan Stamenković Crispin Thurlow Assimakis Tseronis Rebekah Wegener","authors":["Gostrer, Galina"],"language":"de","year":2026,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Multimodal conversational analysis","Virtual meetings","Noticings","Multimodale Konversationsanalyse","Virtuelle Meetings","thema EDItEUR::C Language and Linguistics::CF Linguistics::CFG Semantics, discourse analysis, stylistics","thema EDItEUR::G Reference, Information and Interdisciplinary subjects::GT Interdisciplinary studies::GTC Communication studies","thema EDItEUR::C Language and Linguistics::CF Linguistics","thema EDItEUR::C Language and Linguistics::CJ Language teaching and learning"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116916","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/5ade8329-7ce1-4f36-a6f5-85aa1f8e16db/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/a07ebba5-27ea-4137-90d2-65069dba0c20/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116916","handle":"20.500.12657/116916","dspace_uuid":"f6907b53-9c1f-4754-8ac5-07777ee46aea"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116916","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116916","language":"de","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/a07ebba5-27ea-4137-90d2-65069dba0c20/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:7357727bde869f1bcb91bf02","title":"KI – Körper – Didaktik. Leibliche Dimensionen sprachlichen und literarischen Lernens mit KI","subtitle":"","description":"Generative artificial intelligence is fundamentally transforming linguistic and literary learning processes. What does this development mean for German language instruction, in which learning is tied to the body, perception, emotion, experience, and social relationships? The contributions in this volume combine perspectives from cognitive science, body phenomenology, media theory, and German language pedagogy. They examine differences between human and artificial cognition, transhumanist visions of the future, and the materiality and corporeality of texts. Furthermore, they discuss the physically impactful nature of chatbots and the emotional dynamics in human-AI dialogues. The volume also includes analyses of digital writing, reading instruction, and literary writing. Together, these contributions paint a broad picture of the opportunities, limitations, and tensions surrounding generative AI in German language instruction—with a particular focus on corporeality, materiality, affectivity, co-presence, and aesthetic practice.","authors":[],"language":"de","year":2026,"publisher":"Frank & Timme","isbn":[],"doi":"10.57088/978-3-7329-8686-6","subjects":["Elementary School","Secondary Education","Subject-Specific Pedagogy","Transhumanism","Literature Pedagogy","Artificial Intelligence","Creative Writing","Literature Instruction","Media Pedagogy","AI Sy