}],"access":"public_domain","license":"Public domain in the United States","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:92de873ee1a1e520dd0c3a73","title":"Linux device drivers","subtitle":"","description":"","authors":["Alessandro Rubini","Jonathan Corbet","Greg Kroah-Hartman"],"language":"fr","year":1998,"publisher":"O'Reilly & Associates","isbn":["9781449371616","1449371612","9780596555382","8441518890","0596555385","9780596000080","9788441518896","9780596150297","1565922921","0596517432","0596000081","9780596005900","2841771997","9782841771998","0596005903","9781565922921","0596150296","9780596517434"],"doi":"","subjects":[],"source":"Open Library","source_url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2119947W","cover_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/805706-M.jpg","formats":[],"access":"digital_lending","license":"","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"OL2119947W","openlibrary_work":"OL2119947W","openlibrary_edition":"OL13267944M","internet_archive":"linuxdevicedrive00rubi"},"providers":[{"source":"Open Library","source_id":"OL2119947W","source_url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2119947W","language":"fr","formats":[],"access":"digital_lending","license":"","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:c4085844cbdd55b9a43c23e8","title":"Listening in Radiophonic Spaces","subtitle":"Politics and Aesthetics of Early British Broadcasting","description":"Based on research in the BBC archives, this book examines the negotiation and establishment of a figure of the ideal listener and ideal listening in the context of BBC radio drama in the 1920s. Guided by the idea of itself as a public service, the BBC articulated a new space of aesthetic experience constituted by broadcasting as a public sphere from the very beginning and conceived of its listener as a citizen, characterized by a specifically trained listening and the capacity to imagine and concentrate. If this conception of the listener and his listening shows how the BBC resolutely positioned itself to shape society through education and training, the study exposes British broadcasting as both an aesthetic and a political order. This study turns to the radio drama as the location at which the listening and imaginative competencies required from the listener are short-circuited with strategies of radiophonic sound production and transmission. It illuminates how in theories and practices of BBC radio drama a new realm of possibilities of medial experience was being gauged. In using concrete radiodramatic strategies it shows how British broadcasting forms a specific order by attempting to position and orient the listener within this new radiophonic space. In doing so, the study states, a radiophonic drama conceived by the BBC performs a programmatic transfer of specific British values upheld by the public service into universal categories and blurs the traces of their production not least on the basis of a specifically conditioned listener.","authors":["Gerber, Tobias"],"language":"en","year":2026,"publisher":"Bloomsbury Publishing (US)","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Radiophonic","Audience","Public service","BBC","British broadcasting","Imperialist structures","Value system","Hegemony","Postcolonialism","Identity","Media-aesthetic","Media theory","Radio theory","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AT Performing arts::ATM Film, television, radio and performing arts genres::ATMF Film, television, radio genres: Drama","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AV Music::AVA Theory of music and musicology","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AT Performing arts::ATL Radio / podcasts","thema EDItEUR::N History and Archaeology","thema EDItEUR::3 Time period qualifiers::3M c 1500 onwards to present day::3MP 20th century, c 1900 to c 1999","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AT Performing arts::ATF Films, cinema","thema EDItEUR::A The Arts::AT Performing arts::ATJ Television","thema EDItEUR::P Mathematics and Science::PH Physics::PHD Classical mechanics::PHDS Wave mechanics (vibration and acoustics)"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/117059","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/78736217-da81-4871-b27b-4ba555f16392/retrieve","formats":[],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/117059","handle":"20.500.12657/117059","dspace_uuid":"5c8dafaf-22cf-4865-98fc-8823d4fe539b"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/117059","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/117059","language":"en","formats":[],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ · CC-BY-NC-ND","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:654852d973c712066a03bd4b","title":"Literary Acts of Agency","subtitle":"Negotiating the Rule of Law","description":"At a time when the rule of law is being reshaped and, in certain respects, even severely distorted, this volume examines the ways in which literature both legitimises the principles underlying the present rule of law and anticipates assumptions that take shape before new ideas are publicly felt or articulated within legal discourse. Literary acts of agency thus contribute to a reconfi guration of the legal sphere, advancing arguments through an aesthetic form that remains closely connected to broader social and cultural practices. Rather than approaching literature merely as a vehicle of ruling ideology or as a moral signpost for future legal developments, this volume considers how literary texts negotiate the interval between the rule of law as an ideal and how it is enacted in practice.","authors":[],"language":"en","year":2026,"publisher":"De Gruyter","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["DG Title","thema EDItEUR::J Society and Social Sciences::JB Society and culture: general::JBC Cultural and media studies::JBCC Cultural studies","thema EDItEUR::J Society and Social Sciences::JB Society and culture: general","thema EDItEUR::D Biography, Literature and Literary studies::DS Literature: history and criticism"],"source":"OAPEN Library","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116930","cover_url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/d1a0fc20-da1e-45c5-be8f-0e9d1736a331/retrieve","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/fa106ec3-3b4e-44df-8173-12a513f48b72/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true,"resource_type":"book","identifiers":{"source_id":"20.500.12657/116930","handle":"20.500.12657/116930","dspace_uuid":"0f94b7ae-a530-49c8-9e49-3e5c59a1d187"},"providers":[{"source":"OAPEN Library","source_id":"20.500.12657/116930","source_url":"https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/116930","language":"en","formats":[{"type":"pdf","url":"https://library.oapen.org/rest/bitstreams/fa106ec3-3b4e-44df-8173-12a513f48b72/retrieve"}],"access":"creative_commons","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ · CC-BY · Other open license","verified_legal":true}]},{"id":"work:5e6a90608c4fa64405f5532d","title":"Little Women; Or, Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy","subtitle":"","description":"","authors":["Alcott, Louisa May"],"language":"en","year":null,"publisher":"Project Gutenberg","isbn":[],"doi":"","subjects":["Autobiographical fiction","Bildungsromans","Domestic fiction","Family life -- New England -- Fiction","March family (Fictitious characters) -- Fiction","Mothers and daughters -- Fiction","New England -- Fiction","Sisters -- Fiction","Young women -- Fiction","Category: American Literature","Category: Children & Young Adult Reading","Category: Classics of Literature","Category: Novels"],"source":"Project Gutenberg","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/37106","cover_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37106/pg37106.cover.medium.jpg","formats":[{"type":"text","u